question.:question/problem:
Differentiate $\mathbf f(t)+2\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf f(t)&=\langle t^2,\,3t-2,\,4\rangle \\ \mathbf g(t)&=\langle t,\,1-t^2,\,2t\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

For $t>-1$, find $(2\mathbf f-\mathbf g)\prime(t)$ in component form, where

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf f(t)&=\langle \sqrt{t+1},\,\ln(t+1),\,\cos(2t)\rangle \\ \mathbf g(t)&=\langle \frac{1}{t+1},\,e^{2t},\,\sin(2t)\rangle
\end{aligned}
$$

Find $(2\mathbf f-\mathbf g)\prime(1)$, where, for $t>0$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf f(t)&=\begin{bmatrix}2\sqrt{t+3} \\ \cos(t-1) \\ \ln t\end{bmatrix} \\ \mathbf g(t)&=\begin{bmatrix}t^2 \\ t^3 \\ e^{t-1}\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

question.:question/worked-solution:
Differentiate before substituting the evaluation point. The sum and constant multiple rules give

$$
(2\mathbf f-\mathbf g)\prime(t)=2\mathbf f\prime(t)-\mathbf g\prime(t).
$$

Component by component,

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf f\prime(t)&=\begin{bmatrix}\frac{1}{\sqrt{t+3}} \\ -\sin(t-1) \\ \frac{1}{t}\end{bmatrix} \\ \mathbf g\prime(t)&=\begin{bmatrix}2t \\ 3t^2 \\ e^{t-1}\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluating at $t=1$ gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf f\prime(1)&=\begin{bmatrix}\frac12 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\ \mathbf g\prime(1)&=\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$

Combine the derivative values:

$$
\begin{aligned}
(2\mathbf f-\mathbf g)\prime(1)&=2\begin{bmatrix}\frac12 \\ 0 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}-\begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3 \\ 1\end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}1-2 \\ 0-3 \\ 2-1\end{bmatrix} \\ &=\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\ -3 \\ 1\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
$$

